    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization applied whenever an owner is created, keeping each piece of logic in a small, single-responsibility unit and reusing it across the required-field check and the household comparisons.\n\n**New class \u2014 `AddressNormalizer`** (`rest/controller/AddressNormalizer.java`)\nMirrors the existing `TelephoneNormalizer`/`EmailNormalizer` siblings. Its `normalize` method trims, collapses internal whitespace to single spaces, upper-cases, and expands street-type abbreviations (`ST`\u2192`STREET`, `RD`\u2192`ROAD`, `AVE`\u2192`AVENUE`). A null/blank input yields an empty string so the required-field check can reject it.\n\n**`OwnerFieldsValidator`** \u2014 injected `AddressNormalizer` and now checks the *normalized* address for blankness, so an address that is blank after normalization is rejected (400).\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 injected `AddressNormalizer` and normalize the mapped owner's address once, immediately after mapping and before the household branch. Because the normalized value is what gets stored/returned *and* what feeds `Households` keying, both household duplicate detection and the shared household id derive from the normalized form \u2014 no logic duplicated into the household classes.\n\n**Why this placement:** normalizing on the `Owner` a single time means the stored address, the returned DTO, the duplicate check, and the stable household id all consume the one canonical value, rather than re-deriving it in each collaborator. `updateOwner` was left untouched since the spec scopes this to creation.\n\nTest results: the three new acceptance tests (whitespace/case, abbreviation expansion, blank-after-normalize \u2192 400) and the full suite all pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
